<h2 id="what-the-agents-are-trying-to-learn">What the agents are trying to learn</h2>

<p>ARC-AGI-3 uses interactive environments. Agents must discover goals and dynamics through actions, then carry that understanding across increasingly difficult levels. The benchmark evaluates how efficiently they acquire and apply those skills, with action counts compared against human baselines. <a href="https://arcprize.org/arc-agi/3">ARC Prize’s benchmark overview</a></p>

<p>Both solutions give a language model the means to write and execute Python. That creates a productive division of labor: the model proposes explanations and programs; code measures changes, simulates consequences, and searches possibilities.</p>

<p>The critical engineering question is how to connect those programs to reality. A perfectly executed search can still fail if its simulator encodes the wrong rule. A correct observation can still be lost when the conversation becomes too long.</p>

<h2 id="first-ranked-team-arc-code-as-an-experimental-workspace">First-ranked team: ARC-Code as an experimental workspace</h2>

<p>The <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">arc-code-hs110-click</code> notebook packages an entire agent deployment. Its embedded archive contains the launcher, game middleware, action client, recording system, prompts, reviewer, and supporting code. A pinned local model server supplies the language model.</p>

<p>The core idea is to give each game an independent coding workspace. The agent can inspect the board, write parsers and simulators, run bounded experiments, and keep a record of its conclusions. The notebook’s prompts describe a scientific workflow: make a small prediction, spend a few actions testing it, compare the observations, and revise the explanation.</p>

<p>The code and configuration discussed below come from the <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/code/threerabbits/arc-code-hs110-click/notebook">ARC-Code notebook</a>, including its embedded <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">arcforge.arc_code</code> modules and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">work/arc_code</code> files.</p>

<h3 id="a-game-action-becomes-recorded-evidence">A game action becomes recorded evidence</h3>

<p>The agent interacts through a small command-line interface. It can initialize the game, inspect its status or board, and send individual actions or batches. The supplied interface describes a 64×64 board, with clicks addressed by column and row.</p>

<p>A middleware layer sits between the player and the upstream game gateway. It associates each player’s credentials with one game, checks action availability and coordinates, and records the responses. The upstream gateway remains responsible for game behavior and the scorecard.</p>

<p>This placement matters. If the agent writes its own Python client, its actions still pass through the middleware and enter the record. The agent’s own account of what happened is therefore separate from the observation history.</p>

<p>For each step, the workspace receives the final board as text and an image, the returned frame array, and intermediate images or an animation when the action produces multiple frames. A history entry connects the action to the level, outcome, changed-cell count, and the plan supplied with the batch.</p>

<p>The player is instructed to use images for overall structure and code for exact coordinates and differences. This avoids repeatedly transcribing a board by eye. Intermediate frames can also reveal a mechanism that the final frame hides: an object may move and return, or a click may trigger a sequence whose order matters.</p>

<h3 id="the-click-addition-makes-mistakes-easier-to-interpret">The “click” addition makes mistakes easier to interpret</h3>

<p>The named notebook adds feedback about the cell an <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ACTION6</code> click actually hit. Before sending the click, the client reads the current board and reports the target’s color and the size and bounds of its same-colored connected region.</p>

<p>If the click lands on the inferred background, it looks nearby for another color. After execution, it can also report that the final board did not change.</p>

<p>This helps distinguish two very different explanations for an unproductive action: the agent clicked the intended object and misunderstood its behavior, or it missed the object entirely. Discovering the second case early can prevent a long investigation of a rule that was never actually tested.</p>

<p>The feedback is computed from visible board data. Its description of a region is geometric; it does not reveal the region’s role in the game. Its background label is also a heuristic based on the most frequent color.</p>

<h3 id="memory-lives-in-files-as-well-as-conversation">Memory lives in files as well as conversation</h3>

<p>ARC-Code separates several kinds of memory:</p>

