<p>The record is read-only to the player. The remaining files are working material that the player can revise.</p>

<p>Before context compaction, a reminder asks the agent to update its notes. The compaction prompt separates confirmed mechanics, hypotheses, solved levels, current state, useful files, and the next step. Afterward, the player is instructed to reread its durable notes.</p>

<p>This is more than retaining a transcript. It tries to preserve the distinction between a rule supported by an experiment and an attractive guess. That distinction is especially valuable when an assumption would otherwise be copied into a summary and treated as established knowledge.</p>

<h3 id="reviewers-challenge-beliefs-without-spending-game-actions">Reviewers challenge beliefs without spending game actions</h3>

<p>For stalled games, the launcher can start a separate reviewer. The reviewer receives a snapshot of the action record, notes, and code, with updates supplied while the player continues.</p>

<p>Its task is to identify a small number of consequential beliefs that are contradicted or insufficiently tested. Findings must cite recorded steps or reproducible checks. The reviewer cannot operate the game; it delivers a report, and the player decides how to use it.</p>

<p>This arrangement targets a common failure mode: an agent builds a simulator, searches it unsuccessfully, and concludes that the real game has no solution. The reviewer is prompted to ask whether the simulator’s assumptions were ever justified.</p>

<p>The configuration makes this help conditional on available capacity. In the competition configuration, reviews begin only after two hours, target games stalled for at least 45 minutes, and require player-plus-reviewer occupancy below 64. This does not replace the main launcher’s 110-game concurrency setting; it restricts when additional reviewers can enter.</p>

<h3 id="the-serving-system-supports-many-long-running-players">The serving system supports many long-running players</h3>

<p>The notebook serves the local checkpoint under the name <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">qwen38fn</code>, using a custom SGLang runtime and a patched coding-agent CLI. The CLI is the tool-using driver; requests are routed to the local checkpoint.</p>

<p>The deployment combines low-precision model and cache storage, speculative decoding, and session-cache machinery. These features aim to keep many long conversations affordable. Separately, the launcher adjusts compaction thresholds according to the shared cache budget and the number of running players.</p>

<p>Each game’s processes are also bounded. The configuration sets memory and CPU limits, and a watchdog can stop runaway work. A search that exhausts the machine would otherwise take time and capacity away from every other game.</p>

<h2 id="second-ranked-team-tufas-sealagent-and-executable-world-models">Second-ranked team: Tufa’s SealAgent and executable world models</h2>

<p>Tufa’s notebook uses the Tufa ARC-AGI Framework, or TAAF, to stage a source bundle, prepare inference, load the benchmark, and run its solver. The saved configuration identifies that solver as <strong><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">SealAgent</code></strong>.</p>

<p>The notebook is primarily the deployment entry point. Its linked source bundle contains the actual implementation in the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">seal</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">platypus</code>, and supporting packages. This analysis uses both the notebook’s saved configuration and that bundle, rather than assuming every feature in the repository is enabled. <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/code/jeroencottaar/tufa-labs-arc3-final-solution">Tufa notebook</a> · <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/jeroencottaar/platypus-demo-seal-xkb-keepalive-ds">Linked source bundle</a></p>

<h3 id="a-persistent-python-session-becomes-the-agents-workbench">A persistent Python session becomes the agent’s workbench</h3>

<p>SealAgent provides structured objects for actions, frames, and the history of the current level. The model writes Python in a persistent kernel. Its code can inspect the state, compute a result, submit an action, inspect the returned observation, and continue within the same call.</p>

<p>This reduces the need to convert every useful operation into a separate conversational exchange. A conditional procedure can remain a procedure: inspect a piece, attempt a move, and branch on what actually happened.</p>

<p>In the saved configuration, the kernel persists for a level. Files and journaled knowledge can carry across levels, while level-specific variables are cleared at the boundary. This favors reusing general mechanics without accidentally reusing coordinates or object identities from an earlier board.</p>

<h3 id="perception-includes-structure-and-exact-change-descriptions">Perception includes structure and exact change descriptions</h3>

<p>The agent receives the current grid as an image and can access integer-valued board data in Python. A segmentation helper exposes connected structure for further inspection.</p>

<p>The <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">framediff</code> machinery adds another representation: a short block of NumPy operations that reconstructs a newly observed frame from an available baseline. That baseline may be the previous frame, the level’s initial frame, or an incorrect prediction from the agent’s world model.</p>

<p>The proposed reconstruction is executed and checked before being shown. This gives the model a compact, exact account of a visual difference when one can be found within the search budget. It still has to infer what the difference means.</p>

<p>The saved configuration enables settled-frame differences and per-action change reports, with animation descriptions available on request. It does not enable every visual aid: the separate difference-image option is off.</p>

<h3 id="a-world-model-is-an-executable-hypothesis">A world model is an executable hypothesis</h3>

<p>SealAgent can maintain a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">world_model.py</code> file describing the game’s dynamics. Its interface separates an abstract initial state, a transition function, and a renderer that predicts the visible board. An optional distance-to-goal function can guide search.</p>

<p>This turns a verbal explanation into a program with testable consequences. Instead of merely saying that a colored piece slides until blocked, the agent must specify how its position changes and what the resulting board should look like.</p>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">test_world_model()</code> replays the current level’s recorded history through that program. It reports the first mismatch between a prediction and the observations. A passing replay also supplies the agent’s predicted state at each recorded turn.</p>

<p>Crucially, the supplied configuration uses <strong>optional world models</strong>. An agent without a passing model can still act. The system offers verified simulation as a tool, rather than requiring a complete simulator before any progress is possible.</p>

<p>Passing the test validates the modeled history under the configured comparison rules. It does not establish that the program predicts every unseen situation. Partial rendering and tolerance controls make this distinction important.</p>

<h3 id="search-becomes-useful-once-the-simulator-earns-some-trust">Search becomes useful once the simulator earns some trust</h3>

<p>After a Python call explicitly tests the model successfully and makes no real move, the harness can launch a bounded search in a separate process. It rechecks the model there before planning.</p>

<p>The planner explores simulated transitions. It uses a supplied distance-to-goal heuristic when available and falls back to breadth-first ordering otherwise. It can seek a win or a boundary where the model cannot predict what happens next.</p>

<p>A result is returned as suggested actions and predicted intermediate history. The agent retains the decision to execute it. Search therefore operates inside a larger learning loop: build a hypothesis, check it, explore its consequences, and test those consequences against the game.</p>

<p>While a world model passes, real action sequences are checked step by step. A mismatched prediction stops the remaining sequence and returns control for investigation. Previously executed actions still count; the guard limits further damage rather than undoing the mistake.</p>

<p>Click search also uses a reduced action set. It selects representative cells from connected monochromatic regions, with a configured cap, instead of treating all 4,096 board coordinates as equally useful candidates. Failure to find a plan is consequently a result about the modeled transitions and searched candidates.</p>

<h3 id="reflection-audits-and-compaction-serve-different-purposes">Reflection, audits, and compaction serve different purposes</h3>

<p>SealAgent’s memory system includes a journal, saved files, a level-local Python session, and conversation summaries.</p>

<p>After clearing a level, a reflection turn lets it consolidate what it learned before proceeding. Separately, the saved configuration requests a strategy audit after 300,000 completion tokens on a level. During that turn, acting is disabled and the agent is prompted to reconsider its approach, including neglected actions or click targets.</p>

<p>This differs from ARC-Code’s separate reviewer. Tufa schedules a reconsideration phase in the player’s own control loop; ARC-Code can assign another agent to independently inspect the evidence.</p>

<p>The saved Tufa configuration uses a working context budget of 181,184 tokens and a target of 33,925 after compaction, within an engine ceiling of 262,144. Files, relevant Python state, and explicit knowledge reduce the amount that must survive in the prose summary alone.</p>

<h3 id="compute-allocation-is-part-of-the-solution">Compute allocation is part of the solution</h3>

<p>The configuration permits up to 80 concurrent games and chooses the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">least_game_tokens</code> scheduling policy. The source implements this as priority for games with the lowest accumulated token expenditure, helping distribute service across the game list.</p>

<p>Inference runs through a custom vLLM backend. The saved deployment uses low-precision weights and KV storage, CPU cache offloading, prefix-cache features, and a speculative draft model. These are serving optimizations; the notebook itself loads existing checkpoints and runs inference rather than training the model on the games.</p>

<p>The distinction matters when interpreting “learning.” Here, adaptation can occur through better code, a corrected simulator, a journal entry, or a revised plan. Those changes can improve behavior without updating neural-network weights during the run.</p>

