<p>Both designs make a language model responsible for forming and revising explanations. They also give ordinary software a substantial role in checking details that are difficult to maintain reliably in prose.</p>

<p>ARC-Code emphasizes an auditable experimental workspace. Tufa more explicitly connects a learned simulator to replay tests, planning, and guarded execution. In both, the useful unit of progress is a belief that has survived a relevant test and can be carried into the next decision.</p>

<p>That is a demanding standard. A simulator may fit the past and still fail on a new configuration. A summary may preserve an error. A reviewer may arrive too late. The systems are interesting because their architecture gives those failures places to become visible and correctable.</p>

<h2 id="technical-appendix">Technical appendix</h2>

<h3 id="a-reading-the-first-notebooks-embedded-source">A. Reading the first notebook’s embedded source</h3>

<p>The ARC-Code notebook’s large configuration cell contains a Base64-encoded, gzip-compressed tar archive. The analysis unpacked it as data and checked the hash prefixes of all <strong>539</strong> carried files against its manifest. The important paths are:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">work/arc_code/run_arm.py</code>: orchestration, per-game workspaces, sessions, context budgets, and reviewer integration.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">src/arcforge/arc_code/middleware.py</code>: game-bound access, action validation, recording, and upstream communication.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">src/arcforge/arc_code/act_client.py</code>: the generated action client and click feedback.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">src/arcforge/arc_code/record.py</code>: text boards, images, frame arrays, and animations.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">work/arc_code/prompts/v14/</code>: the active player brief and compaction prompts.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">work/arc_code/review_scheduler.py</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">review_prompts/r7/</code>: review admission and the active review instructions.</li>
</ul>

<p>The archive contains other versions and optional features. The two <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">*-review-click.json</code> configurations identify the paths relevant to this notebook.</p>

<h3 id="b-context-capacity-is-not-one-number">B. Context capacity is not one number</h3>

<p>ARC-Code configures a 240,000-token server context, a 235,000-token driver context, and a 150,000-token automatic-compaction cap. Its dynamic threshold is the safety-adjusted share of the server cache pool per active player, clamped between 100,000 tokens and that cap. A separate reminder helps save notes before compaction.</p>

<p>These values serve different purposes. The server ceiling constrains a request; the driver budget reserves room around it; compaction governs how much history remains active under shared load.</p>

<p>Tufa similarly separates its 262,144-token engine ceiling, 181,184-token working context, and 33,925-token compaction target. Comparing only the largest context numbers would miss how both systems actually manage conversations.</p>

<h3 id="c-scoring-rewards-completion-and-action-efficiency">C. Scoring rewards completion and action efficiency</h3>

<p>The ARC-Code scorer gives a cleared level an efficiency value of <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">min(100 * (baseline_actions / agent_actions) ** 2, 115)</code>. It weights levels by their one-based index and includes every level’s weight in the denominator, including uncleared levels. A further cap limits the game’s score by the weighted share of levels completed.</p>

<p>Consequently, solving early levels efficiently cannot fully compensate for never reaching later ones. Exploration can be worthwhile when it unlocks progress, but repeated actions that provide no new information reduce efficiency.</p>

<h3 id="d-where-tufas-logic-lives">D. Where Tufa’s logic lives</h3>

<p>The linked source archive identifies the submission repository snapshot as <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">8863e380</code>. Within <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">packages/seal/src/seal/</code>:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">agent.py</code> defines the playing loop, active configuration fields, context handling, reflection, and audits.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">harness/game.py</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">harness/test.py</code> connect Python actions and world-model checks to the recorded level history.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">frame_delta.py</code> builds and verifies compact frame reconstructions.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">search.py</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">search_process.py</code>, and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">planner.py</code> run bounded planning and report its provenance.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">move_lister.py</code> selects candidate actions, including representative clicks.</li>
</ul>

<p>The saved configuration enables optional world models and search, with a 130-second search budget and 2 GiB of additional search-process memory. The planner’s name is <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">BFSPlanner</code>, but it uses best-first ordering when a usable heuristic is provided; breadth-first ordering is its fallback.</p>

<p>The configuration also has <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">expose_undo=False</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">confine_filesystem=False</code>. The presence of undo or Landlock confinement code in the package therefore does not establish that those features operated in this saved run.</p>

<h3 id="e-reproduction-and-interpretation">E. Reproduction and interpretation</h3>

<p>Both notebooks depend on mounted model checkpoints, packaged runtimes, and competition infrastructure. Their visible top-level cells are deployment programs rather than self-contained solver algorithms that run unchanged in an ordinary Python environment.</p>

<p>ARC-Code switches between a public stand-in and the competition gateway. Tufa loads its bundled benchmark in demonstration mode and obtains the game list from the competition gateway during a true rerun. Both write a placeholder submission in non-rerun operation; that file’s dummy score is not evidence of game performance.</p>

<p>The attached ARC-Code export also contains configuration notes dated October 27 while its saved execution metadata dates the run to October 26. Its output should therefore not be treated as an ablation proving the effect of the added click feedback.</p>

